    "available_at": "2026-10-16T14:29:30.520656+00:00",
    "body": "Most supply chain leaders say their employees are unprepared to use AI, according to a new report from the Association for Supply Chain Management ( ASCM ) and the Digital Supply Chain Institute ( DSCI ). The study found that 83% of respondents say their workers aren’t ready to use AI effectively, even as their companies plan to make it part of everyday work.\nWhat’s Related\nThe joint study, The People Paradox in Supply Chain AI Integration , found that 91% of organizations plan to use AI to help human workers, while just 7% expect it to directly replace them.\nResearchers collected 144 survey responses and used 107 for detailed analysis. Respondents included managers, directors, executives, and staff, with about 62% based in North America.\n“AI adoption in supply chain is more about people than it is technology ,” said ASCM CEO Abe Eshkenazi. “The organizations making real progress treat workforce readiness as part of the technology investment, not an afterthought. They learn by doing, bring managers into the process early, and build trust one function at a time.”\nRELATED STORIES: Half of Supply Chain Leaders Lack Talent to Run AI Tools | Why So Few Supply Chains Have Fully Embraced AI\nManagers know about AI projects but often aren’t involved\nThe report found that half of directors, 51% of managers, and 70% of staff knew about their organizations’ AI initiatives without actively participating. Another 20% of staff reported no involvement at all.\nWorkforce skills and resources tied to an unclear business case or return on investment were the most frequently cited barriers, each at 33%. Poor data quality followed at 25%, while change management and governance or compliance risks each drew 21%.\nCompanies that described their workforce as prepared reported a 71% AI project success rate, compared with 28% among unprepared organizations. The prepared group had used AI across an average of six supply chain functions, while roughly 64% of unprepared organizations remained in planning or pilot stages.\nPlanning leads, but AI rarely makes decisions on its own\nAcross 13 supply chain functions, average AI adoption scored 1.94 on a five-point scale. A score of one meant no AI use, while five meant AI could act without human review.\nDemand planning led at 2.28, followed by supply and inventory planning at 2.12. Reverse logistics ranked last at 1.59, and 55% of respondents described their AI projects as somewhat successful.\nThe report recommends involving directors and managers early in tool selection and work process design. It also calls for starting with a manageable project with clean data, a responsible owner and measurable results.\n“With tariff shifts, demand shocks and geopolitical disruption , the advantage will go to companies that can sense and re-plan fastest. That makes AI readiness a management challenge first and a technology challenge second,” said Marko Kovacevic, managing director of DSCI.",
